  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how stereocomplex formation between polymer chains can be used to build well-defined nanostructures. At the top, two examples of complementary polymer pairs are shown: PDLA with PLLA, which forms a thermally robust stereocomplex, and isotactic and syndiotactic PMMA, which form an optically active stereocomplex. The drawings emphasize that chains with different stereochemistry can recognize and bind one another in an ordered way.\n\nThis stereocomplexation is then presented as a design principle for assembling larger structures. In the lower part, the paired polymers appear to organize into highly regular cylindrical objects with a central red core. One rendering shows a long rod-like or fiber-like assembly in perspective, while the other shows its circular cross-section. In both views, blue and orange polymer segments are packed in concentric or radially arranged layers around the core, suggesting a controlled, repeating internal architecture rather than a disordered aggregate.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that stereospecific interactions between polymer chains can drive self-assembly into stable, defined nanoscale morphologies with precise structural organization.",
